ll of them. Therefore, regardless, you can partition the 10-element subset into a group with 15 and four evens (coprime if evens are not multiples of 3 or 5) and the rest as non-coprime. Therefore, even adding a composite odd number allows the formation of such a 10-element subset.\n\nTherefore, once we exceed 49 elements, any subset of size 50 must contain at least one odd number, and we can form a 10-element subset with that odd and nine evens, which can be partitioned to satisfy the conditions. Therefore, the maximal m is 49, and the minimal n is 50.\n\nBut wait, the problem is in Chinese, so the set S is up to 98. The number of even numbers is 49 (2, 4, ..., 98). Therefore, if we take a subset of size 50, it must contain at least one odd number. Now, regardless of whether that odd number is prime or composite, we can select a 10-element subset consisting of this odd number and nine evens. If the odd number is prime, then in any partition where the prime is grouped with four evens, the prime is coprime with them (since evens are not multiples of the prime, assuming the prime is not 2). If the odd number is composite, say 15, then if we select evens not divisible by 3 or 5, 15 is coprime with them. If we select evens divisible by 3 or 5, then 15 is non-coprime. Therefore, depending on the evens chosen, we might have different scenarios.\n\nBut the key is that in the subset of 50 elements, there exists at least one 10-element subset that can be partitioned to meet the conditions. Therefore, the question is whether in any subset of 50 elements containing at least one odd number, we can always find a 10-element subset of the form {odd, nine evens}. However, this is not necessarily the case. The subset of 50 elements could contain multiple odds, but if the evens are chosen carefully, but given that there are 49 evens, adding one odd gives 50 elements. If more odds are added, say two odds, then we have 51 elements (49 evens + 2 odds), but the same logic applies: we can