en number of turns, the cups in any pair will either be both full or both empty due to symmetry.\n\n    - Thus, the game will proceed until all cups are filled. Because $n$ is even, after Vasya's turn, it would mean he fills the last pair of cups, leaving Petya without a move. Therefore, Vasya wins when $n$ is even.\n\n4. **Case for Odd $n$**: Now consider the case when $n$ is odd.\n\n    - In this case, the number of pairs of cups is $n$, which is odd.\n    - The strategy for Petya can be derived as follows:\n        - Petya starts by filling cups from a specific pair.\n        - After this initial move, there will be an even number of pairs of empty cups remaining.\n\n    - The game is now reduced to a similar situation as when $n$ is even, but with roles switched:\n        - Using the strategy described for even $n$, Petya can now ensure that he always fills cups symmetrically or adjacent, leaving an even number of empty pairs each turn he plays.\n    \n    - Since there is now an even number of pairs, Petya's usage of Vasya's strategy with positions switched means Petya will always have a move and force Vasya to lose by leaving Vasya without a move. Therefore, Petya wins when $n$ is odd.\n\n5. **Conclusion**: Based on the analysis, Petya wins if $n$ is odd regardless of Vasya's actions.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Petya wins for odd } n}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all functions \\( f: \\mathbb{R} \\longrightarrow \\mathbb{R} \\) such that \\( f \\) is monotone and there exists an \\( n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\) such that for all \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ f^{n}(x) = -x \\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all monotone functions f from the real numbers to the real numbers such that when you apply the function n times, you get negative x. That is, f^n(x) = -x for all x. Hmm, let me start breaking this down.\n\nFirst, let's make sure I understand the problem correctly. The function f h