etween sides AB and AC. The external angle is the supplement of that, so 180 - 60 = 120 degrees. The external angle bisector would bisect this 120-degree angle. The direction of this bisector can be found using the angle bisector theorem for external angles.\n\nAlternatively, in coordinates, for an equilateral triangle, let's compute the external angle bisectors.\n\nAt vertex A(0,0):\n\nThe sides adjacent to A are AB (from A(0,0) to B(1,0)) and AC (from A(0,0) to C(0.5, sqrt(3)/2)). The external angle bisector at A would be the line that bisects the angle formed by the extension of AB beyond A (i.e., the negative x-axis) and the extension of AC beyond A (i.e., beyond (0,0) in the direction opposite to C). Wait, AC goes from A to C, so the extension beyond A would be the line from A in the direction opposite to C. But C is at (0.5, sqrt(3)/2), so the opposite direction would be towards (-0.5, -sqrt(3)/2). But that might complicate things.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using vector methods. The external angle bisector direction can be determined by the formula for external angle bisector between two lines.\n\nGiven two lines with direction vectors, the external angle bisector direction is given by the normalized difference of the direction vectors. Wait, for internal angle bisector, it's the sum, and for external, it's the difference.\n\nWait, if we have two lines with direction vectors u and v, then the internal angle bisector direction is u/|u| + v/|v|, and the external angle bisector direction is u/|u| - v/|v|. So perhaps applying that.\n\nAt vertex A, the two sides are AB (along the x-axis) and AC (60 degrees above x-axis). The direction vectors for AB is (1,0) and for AC is (0.5, sqrt(3)/2). The external angle bisector direction would be (1,0)/1 - (0.5, sqrt(3)/2)/1 = (0.5, -sqrt(3)/2). So the external bisector at A would be a line starting at A(0,0) in the direction (0.5, -sqrt(3)/2). Similarly, we can compute the external bisectors at B and C.\n\nWait, but in the cas