fes, there are hippos.\n3. In all zoos where there are hippos and giraffes, there are rhinos.\n\nOur goal is to determine whether a zoo can exist that contains hippos but does not contain giraffes or rhinos.\n\nLet's break down each condition:\n\n**Condition 1:** If a zoo has hippos and rhinos, then it cannot have giraffes:\n$$ (H \\cap R) \\cap G = \\emptyset. $$\n\n**Condition 2:** If a zoo has rhinos and does not have giraffes, then it must have hippos:\n$$ R \\cap \\overline{G} \\subseteq H. $$\n\n**Condition 3:** If a zoo has hippos and giraffes, then it must also have rhinos:\n$$ H \\cap G \\subseteq R. $$\n\nWe want to find out if there can exist a zoo that has hippos but does not have giraffes or rhinos:\n$$ H \\cap \\overline{G} \\cap \\overline{R} \\neq \\emptyset. $$\n\nTo investigate, let us consider the implications of the first two conditions in more detail:\n\n**Implication from Condition 1:**\n$$ H \\cap R \\subseteq \\overline{G}. $$\n\nThat is, any zoo that contains both hippos and rhinos must not contain giraffes.\n\n**Implication from Condition 2:**\n$$ R \\cap \\overline{G} \\subseteq H. $$\n\nThis states that any zoo that has rhinos but no giraffes must have hippos.\n\n**Implication from Condition 3:**\n$$ H \\cap G \\subseteq R. $$\n\nThis means that any zoo that contains both hippos and giraffes must also contain rhinos.\n\nCombining these implications, recognize that they do not directly restrict the existence of a zoo that contains only hippos. The conditions only specify combinations including rhinos and/or giraffes.\n\nThus, considering these implications, it does not preclude that there could be a zoo with only hippos (without giraffes and rhinos).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nYes, such a zoo can exist, and it is consistent with the given conditions. Therefore, we conclude:\n\n$$ \\boxed{\\text{Yes, a zoo with hippos but without giraffes and rhinos can exist.}} $$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": 