extending to infinity, but distances involving D' would be problematic. Maybe this is not the right approach.\n\nAlternatively, using inversion to make three points colinear. Let me recall that inversion can map a circle passing through the center of inversion to a line. So, if we have three points not colinear, we can choose an inversion that maps the circle through three of them to a line, but since the original four points are not concyclic, the fourth point would not lie on that circle. So, if we invert with respect to a point on the circle through three points, then that circle becomes a line, and the fourth point is not on that line. Then, Ptolemy's inequality would apply to the three colinear points and the fourth off the line.\n\nWait, let's try this. Suppose we have four points A, B, C, D not concyclic. Then, there exists a circle passing through any three of them, say A, B, C, but D is not on that circle. Let me invert the figure with respect to a point on the circle ABC. Let's choose the center of inversion as point A. Then, the circle ABC (passing through A, B, C) becomes a line (since inversion maps circles through the center to lines). So, after inversion, points A (center) remains, B' and C' lie on a line (the image of circle ABC), and D' is not on that line because D was not on circle ABC (since the original four points are not concyclic). Therefore, in the inverted plane, A is the center, B' and C' are on a line, and D' is some point not on that line. \n\nNow, in this configuration, the quadrilateral is A, B', C', D', with B', C' colinear. Let's consider the triangle inequality here. If we can apply Ptolemy's theorem here, but since three points are colinear, Ptolemy's theorem becomes an equality for a degenerate quadrilateral. Wait, but D' is not on the line B'C', so the quadrilateral is A, B', C', D', with B', C' colinear.\n\nWait, in such a case, Ptolemy's theorem for a convex quadrilateral with three colinear points. Hmm, actually, if three poin