nnot. Hence, adjacent high numbers don't help; they actually reduce the possible number of local maxima.\n\nAnother approach: maybe arranging the numbers in a way where some local maxima are spaced with one knight in between instead of two. But in a circle, spacing them with one knight would mean they are every other seat, which is what the alternating high-low does. If you try to place them closer, they would overlap in their neighbor requirements.\n\nAlternatively, what if we have a more complex pattern, like high, medium, low, high, medium, low, etc.? Maybe the medium numbers can act as local maxima relative to the lows but not the highs. Let's try an example. Suppose we arrange the numbers in descending order but interleaved with medium and low. For instance:\n\n12 (high), 6 (medium), 1 (low), 11 (high), 5 (medium), 2 (low), 10 (high), 4 (medium), 3 (low), 9 (high), 7 (medium), 8 (low). But this might not work. Let's check:\n\n- Knight 12: neighbors 8 and 6. 12>8 and 12>6. True.\n- Knight 6: neighbors 12 and 1. 6 <12 and 6>1. Only one. False.\n- Knight 1: neighbors 6 and 11. 1 <6 and 1 <11. False.\n- Knight 11: neighbors 1 and 5. 11>1 and 11>5. True.\n- Knight 5: neighbors 11 and 2. 5 <11 and 5>2. Only one. False.\n- Knight 2: neighbors 5 and 10. 2 <5 and 2 <10. False.\n- Knight 10: neighbors 2 and 4. 10>2 and 10>4. True.\n- Knight 4: neighbors 10 and 3. 4 <10 and 4>3. Only one. False.\n- Knight 3: neighbors 4 and 9. 3 <4 and 3 <9. False.\n- Knight 9: neighbors 3 and 7. 9>3 and 9>7. True.\n- Knight 7: neighbors 9 and 8. 7 <9 and 7 <8. False.\n- Knight 8: neighbors 7 and 12. 8 <12 and 8>7. Only one. False.\n\nIn this arrangement, the true claims are knights 12,11,10,9. That's four, which is worse than six. So this approach doesn't help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe if we have high numbers separated by two low numbers. For example: high, low, low, high, low, low, etc. Let's try:\n\n12,1,2,11,3,4,10,5,6,9,7,8.\n\nCheck each:\n\n- 12: neighbors 8 and 1. 12>8 and 12>1. True.\n- 1: neighbors 12 and 2. 1 <12 and 1 <2. False.\n- 2: neighbors 1 and 11. 2 <1 (no, wait 1 <2) neighbors 1 and 11: 2 <11 and 2>1. Only one. False.\n- 11: neighbors 2 and 3. 11>2 and 11>3. True.\n- 3: neighbors 11 and 4. 3 <11 and 3 <4. False.\n- 4: neighbors 3 and 10. 4 <10 and 4>3. Only one. False.\n- 10: neighbors 4 and 5. 10>4 and 10>5. True.\n- 5: neighbors 10 and 6. 5 <10 and 5 <6. False.\n- 6: neighbors 5 and 9. 6 <9 and 6>5. Only one. False.\n- 9: neighbors 6 and 7. 9>6 and 9>7. True.\n- 7: neighbors 9 and 8. 7 <9 and 7 <8. False.\n- 8: neighbors 7 and 12. 8 <12 and 8>7. Only one. False.\n\nTrue claims: 12,11,10,9. Again four. Not better. So this approach also doesn't yield more than six. It seems that the alternating high-low pattern is indeed the optimal.\n\nBut wait, another thought: in the alternating high-low arrangement, each high is followed by a low, which is then followed by a high. This creates a situation where every high is surrounded by two lows. However, the lows are arranged in increasing order. For example, in the sequence 12,1,11,2,10,3..., the lows are 1,2,3,4,5,6. Each low is placed between two high numbers, and they increase as you go around the circle. This ensures that each high number is greater than both its neighboring lows. Since the lows are increasing, the next high number after a low is always lower than the previous high. For instance, after 12 comes 1, then 11. 11 is less than 12 but greater than 1 and the next low, 2. This pattern continues, ensuring that each high is greater than its immediate lows but less than the previous high. This way, each high can be a local maximum without interfering with the next high.\n\nBut why can't we have more highs? Suppose we try to place seven highs. Let's say we want seven knights to be local maxima. Each of these seven would need to have two neighbors lower than them. But since there are 12 knights, if seven are highs, that would require 14 neighbors (7*2), but each neighbor is shared 