\\boxed{-11}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's consider the given linear functions \\( f(x) = ax + b \\) and \\( g(x) = ax + c \\), where \\( a \\neq 0 \\). The problem states that the graphs of \\( y = f(x) \\) and \\( y = g(x) \\) are parallel lines, which implies they have the same slope, confirming that their slopes \\(a\\) are indeed equal and non-zero. Since these lines are not parallel to the coordinate axes, \\(a \\neq 0\\).\n\n2. The fact that the graph of \\( y = (f(x))^2 \\) touches the graph \\( y = 11g(x) \\) implies that the equation \\( (f(x))^2 = 11g(x) \\) has exactly one solution. This means the quadratic equation derived from \\( (ax + b)^2 = 11(ax + c) \\):\n   \\[\n   (ax + b)^2 = 11(ax + c)\n   \\]\n   can be expanded as:\n   \\[\n   a^2x^2 + 2abx + b^2 = 11ax + 11c\n   \\]\n   Rearranging this, we get:\n   \\[\n   a^2x^2 + 2abx + b^2 - 11ax - 11c = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we obtain:\n   \\[\n   a^2x^2 + a(2b - 11)x + (b^2 - 11c) = 0\n   \\]\n\n3. For this quadratic equation to have exactly one solution, its discriminant \\( \\Delta \\) must be zero. The discriminant of the quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by \\( \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac \\). Applying this to our equation:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = [a(2b - 11)]^2 - 4a^2(b^2 - 11c)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = a^2(2b - 11)^2 - 4a^2(b^2 - 11c)\n   \\]\n   Factoring out \\( a^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = a^2[(2b - 11)^2 - 4(b^2 - 11c)]\n   \\]\n   Setting \\( \\Delta \\) to zero:\n   \\[\n   (2b - 11)^2 - 4(b^2 - 11c) = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   4b^2 - 44b + 121 - 4b^2 + 44c = 0\n   \\]\n   Which reduces to:\n   \\[\n   44c - 44b + 121 = 0\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( b \\) and \\( c \\):\n   \\[\n   4c - 4b = 11\n   \\]\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   c = b + \\frac{11}{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. Similarly, the graph of \\( y = (g(x))^2 \\) touching \\( y = Af(x) \\) implies that the equation \\((g(x))^2 = Af(x) \\) has exactly one solution. Substituting \\( g(x) = ax + c \\) and \\( f(x) = ax + b \\):\n   \\[\n   (ax + c)^2 = A(ax + b)\n   \\]\n   Expanding:\n   \\[\n   a^2x^2 + 2acx + c^2 = Aax + Ab\n   \\]\n   Rearranging:\n   \\[\n   a^2x^2 + 2acx + c^2 - Aax - Ab = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. The discriminant of this quadratic equation must be zero for the equation to have exactly one solution:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = [a(2c - A)]^2 - 4a^2(c^2 - Ab)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = a^2(2c - A)^2 - 4a^2(c^2 - Ab)\n   \\]\n   Factoring out \\( a^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = a^2[(2c - A)^2 - 4(c^2 - Ab)]\n   \\]\n   Setting \\( \\Delta \\) to zero:\n   \\[\n   (2c - A)^2 - 4(c^2 - Ab) = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   4c^2 - 4Ac + A^2 - 4c^2 + 4Ab = 0\n   \\]\n   Which reduces to:\n   \\[\n   A^2 + 4Ab - 4Ac = 0\n   \\]\n   Factoring out:\n   \\[\n   A(A + 4b - 4c) = 0\n   \\]\n   Setting each factor to zero:\n   \\[\n   A = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad A + 4b - 4c = 0\n   \\]\n   From \\( A + 4b - 4c = 0 \\), and using \\( c = b + \\frac{11}{4} \\):\n   \\[\n   A + 4b - 4(b + \\frac{11}{4}) = 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   A + 4b - 4b - 11 = 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   A = -11\n   \\]\n\n6. Conclusion:\n   Thus, the values of \\( A \\) for which the graph \\( y = (g(x))^2 \\) touches the graph \\( y = Af(x) \\) are \\( \\boxed{0} \\) and \\( \\boxed{-11} \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A soccer team has won 60% of the 45 matches played so far. What is the minimum number of matches that this team still needs to win to reach a winning percentage of 75%?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The problem is about a soccer team that has won 60% of their 45 matches so far. We need to find out the minimum number of additional matches they need to win to reach a 75% winning percentage. Hmm, percentage problems can sometimes be tricky, so I need to take it step by step.\n\nFirst, le