at must be divisible by 101. Therefore, the number formed by the first 8 digits (I N N O P O L I) must be divisible by 101.\n\nSo, in summary, the problem reduces to assigning digits I, N, O, P, L, S (with S=0) such that:\n\n1. All letters I, N, O, P, L, S are distinct digits.\n\n2. S = 0.\n\n3. The number formed by the first 8 digits (I N N O P O L I) is divisible by 101.\n\nAdditionally, since I is the first digit, I cannot be 0. Also, S is 0, so none of the other letters can be 0.\n\nSo, the letters to assign digits are:\n\nI, N, O, P, L, S, with S=0. So digits assigned must be unique from 0-9, with S fixed as 0, and the rest (I, N, O, P, L) must be distinct digits from 1-9 (since I cannot be 0, and S is already 0, so others can't be 0 either).\n\nNow, the main challenge is to check if there's a way to assign digits to I, N, O, P, L (all distinct, none zero) such that the 8-digit number I N N O P O L I is divisible by 101.\n\nLet me denote the number as follows:\n\nLet me write the number as D1 D2 D3 D4 D5 D6 D7 D8, where:\n\nD1 = I\n\nD2 = N\n\nD3 = N\n\nD4 = O\n\nD5 = P\n\nD6 = O\n\nD7 = L\n\nD8 = I\n\nSo the number is D1 D2 D3 D4 D5 D6 D7 D8.\n\nThis is an 8-digit number. Let's denote this number as N = D1*10^7 + D2*10^6 + D3*10^5 + D4*10^4 + D5*10^3 + D6*10^2 + D7*10 + D8.\n\nWe need N \u2261 0 mod 101.\n\nAlternatively, since 100 \u2261 -1 mod 101, so 10^2 \u2261 -1 mod 101, which might help in simplifying the expression.\n\nLet me compute N modulo 101 using the property that 10^2 \u2261 -1 mod 101. Therefore:\n\n10^0 \u2261 1\n\n10^1 \u2261 10\n\n10^2 \u2261 -1\n\n10^3 \u2261 -10\n\n10^4 \u2261 1\n\n10^5 \u2261 10\n\n10^6 \u2261 -1\n\n10^7 \u2261 -10\n\nTherefore, the value of N modulo 101 can be expressed as:\n\nD1*(-10) + D2*(-1) + D3*10 + D4*1 + D5*(-10) + D6*(-1) + D7*10 + D8*1\n\nSimplify this expression:\n\n= (-10 D1) + (-1 D2) + 10 D3 + 1 D4 + (-10 D5) + (-1 D6) + 10 D7 + 1 D8\n\nGrouping terms:\n\n= (-10 D1 - D2) + (10 D3 + D4) + (-10 D5 - D6) + (10