le range of the left-hand side is:\n\nMinimum possible value: 9*(-8) + 10*(-8) = -72 -80 = -152\n\nMaximum possible value: 9*(8) + 10*(8) = 72 +80 = 152\n\nSo the possible values range from -152 to +152. Since we need this to be congruent to 0 mod 101, the actual possible values could be -152, -151, ..., 0, ..., 151, 152. But modulo 101, each of these is equivalent to some value between 0 and 100. So we can adjust the equation to find when the left-hand side is 0 mod 101.\n\nGiven that the left-hand side must be 0 modulo 101, we can write:\n\n9(N - I) + 10(L - P) = 101k, where k is some integer. Given the range of possible values for the left-hand side, k can be -1, 0, or 1, because 101*1=101, which is within the max of 152, and 101*(-1)=-101, which is within the min of -152.\n\nSo possible k values: -1, 0, 1.\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n9(N - I) + 10(L - P) = -101, 0, or 101\n\nWe can now consider these three cases.\n\nCase 1: 9(N - I) + 10(L - P) = 0\n\nCase 2: 9(N - I) + 10(L - P) = 101\n\nCase 3: 9(N - I) + 10(L - P) = -101\n\nNow, let's analyze each case.\n\nStarting with Case 1: 9(N - I) + 10(L - P) = 0\n\nThis simplifies to 9(N - I) = -10(L - P)\n\nOr 9(N - I) = 10(P - L)\n\nSince 9 and 10 are coprime, this implies that 10 divides (N - I), and 9 divides (P - L). Let me see:\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nLet\u2019s write 9(N - I) = 10(P - L)\n\nSince 9 and 10 are coprime, 10 must divide (N - I), and 9 must divide (P - L).\n\nBut (N - I) is the difference between two distinct digits (N and I), so it can range from -8 to 8 (excluding 0 since N \u2260 I). Similarly, (P - L) ranges from -8 to 8, excluding 0.\n\nBut if 10 divides (N - I), then (N - I) must be 0, which is impossible because N \u2260 I. Therefore, the only possibility is that (N - I) = 0, which is not allowed. Hence, Case 1 is impossible.\n\nWait, that seems contradictory. Wait, if 9(N - I) = 10(P - L), and since 10 and 9 are coprime, then 9 divides (P - L), and 10 divides (N - I). But since (N