ilarly, let's denote x = N - I and y = L - P.\n\nThen:\n\n9x + 10y = -101\n\nAgain, x and y are integers in [-8,8], excluding 0.\n\nSolve for y:\n\n10y = -101 -9x\n\ny = (-101 -9x)/10\n\nFor y to be integer, (-101 -9x) must be divisible by 10.\n\nTherefore:\n\n-101 -9x \u22610 mod10\n\n=> -101 \u22619x mod10\n\n-101 mod10 = -1, since 101 mod10=1, so -101 mod10= -1 mod10=9\n\nTherefore:\n\n9 \u22619x mod10\n\nDivide both sides by 9 (since 9 and10 are coprime):\n\n1 \u2261x mod10\n\nTherefore, x \u22611 mod10\n\nBut x is in [-8,8], so possible x=1 or x= -9, but -9 is out of range. So x=1.\n\nThus, x=1.\n\nThen, substituting x=1 into the equation:\n\n9(1) +10y = -101\n\n=> 9 +10y = -101\n\n=>10y= -110\n\n=> y= -11\n\nBut y must be between -8 and8. y=-11 is out of range. Therefore, no solution in Case3.\n\nTherefore, Cases 2 and 3 lead to no solutions. Hence, there are no solutions where the equation 9(N - I)+10(L - P) is equal to 101 or -101.\n\nBut wait, this suggests that there are no solutions, which would mean that it's impossible to replace the letters in INNOPOLIS with digits to get a number divisible by 1010.\n\nBut this seems counterintuitive. Maybe I made a mistake in the modular arithmetic or the setup.\n\nLet me double-check the earlier steps.\n\nFirst, we derived that the condition for divisibility by 101 is:\n\n9(N - I) +10(L - P) \u22610 mod101.\n\nThen, considering possible values of the left-hand side between -152 and 152, we considered cases where it's equal to 0, 101, or -101.\n\nBut perhaps there's another approach. Maybe instead of considering the equation 9(N - I) +10(L - P) \u22610 mod101, we can look for values such that this congruence holds, which might not require the left-hand side to be exactly 101 or -101, but could be any multiple of 101. However, given the range of possible values (-152 to 152), the possible multiples of 101 are -101, 0, 101. But since the left-hand side must be congruent to 0 mod101, it can be 0, 101, or -101 modulo 101,