olve for \\( c \\) by dividing both sides by 2.5:\n   \\[\n   c = \\frac{200}{2.5}\n   \\]\n\n8. Calculate the final value:\n   \\[\n   c = 80\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{80}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In the triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), \\( G \\) is the centroid and \\( I \\) is the incenter. Prove that among \\( \\triangle AGI, \\triangle BGI, \\triangle CGI \\), the area of the largest one equals the sum of the areas of the other two.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this geometry problem here. It says in triangle ABC, G is the centroid and I is the incenter. I need to prove that among the triangles AGI, BGI, and CGI, the area of the largest one equals the sum of the areas of the other two. Hmm, interesting. Let me start by recalling what centroids and incenters are.\n\nFirst, the centroid G of a triangle is the intersection point of the medians. It divides each median into a ratio of 2:1, with the longer part being closer to the vertex. So, for example, if we take the median from A to the midpoint of BC, then AG is twice as long as the segment from the midpoint to G.\n\nThe incenter I is the point where the angle bisectors of the triangle meet. It's also the center of the incircle, which touches all three sides of the triangle. The incenter is equidistant from all sides, and its position depends on the angles and sides of the triangle.\n\nNow, the problem is about the areas of triangles AGI, BGI, and CGI. I need to show that the largest area among these three is equal to the sum of the other two. That seems like a relation similar to the Pythagorean theorem, but in this case, it's about areas related through centroid and incenter. Hmm. I need to find a way to relate the areas of these three triangles.\n\nLet me visualize triangle ABC with centroid G and incenter I. The points AGI, BGI, and CGI are each triangles formed by one vertex, the centroid, and the incenter