k to the vector expression for GI:\n\nGI = [ (2a - b - c )A + ( -a + 2b - c )B + ( -a - b + 2c )C ] / [3(a + b + c ) ]\n\nIf we express this as a linear combination of vectors A, B, C, the coefficients are:\n\nFor A: (2a - b - c ) / [3(a + b + c ) ]\n\nFor B: ( -a + 2b - c ) / [3(a + b + c ) ]\n\nFor C: ( -a - b + 2c ) / [3(a + b + c ) ]\n\nNow, the sum of the coefficients is:\n\n(2a - b - c - a + 2b - c - a - b + 2c ) / [3(a + b + c ) ]\n\n= (2a - a - a + (-b + 2b - b ) + (-c - c + 2c )) / denominator\n\n= (0 + 0 + 0 ) / denominator = 0\n\nSo, the vector GI is a linear combination of A, B, C with coefficients summing to zero. This means that GI can be expressed as a combination of the vectors AB and AC, for example.\n\nBut how does this help?\n\nAlternatively, note that if we consider the three areas S_A, S_B, S_C corresponding to AGI, BGI, CGI, these areas are related to the cross products of vectors AG, BG, CG with GI.\n\nSpecifically, S_A = 1/2 | AG \u00d7 GI |, S_B = 1/2 | BG \u00d7 GI |, S_C = 1/2 | CG \u00d7 GI |.\n\nTherefore, we need to show that the maximum of S_A, S_B, S_C is equal to the sum of the other two.\n\nThis would require that the maximum of | AG \u00d7 GI |, | BG \u00d7 GI |, | CG \u00d7 GI | is equal to the sum of the other two.\n\nBut in general, for three real numbers, the maximum being equal to the sum of the other two implies that one of them is the sum of the other two, and all are non-negative. So, if we can show that one of the cross product magnitudes is equal to the sum of the other two, then the corresponding area would satisfy the required property.\n\nBut why would this be true for these particular vectors?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a relation among the vectors AG, BG, CG, and GI that allows this.\n\nSince G is the centroid, we have AG + BG + CG = 0. Because the centroid is the average, so the vectors from the centroid to the vertices sum to zero.\n\nWait, actually, vectors from the centroid to the vertices: AG = G - A, BG = 