o-digit (9+72)=9+81=90. Which is correct, since there are 90 two-digit numbers (from 10 to 99). Wait, but 10 to 99 is 90 numbers. Monodigit two-digit numbers are 9 (11,22,...,99). Then the rest 81 are two-digit numbers using two different digits. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSimilarly, in our calculation:\n\nFor n=2:\n\nMonodigit: 9\n\nCase1: 9 numbers (10,20,...,90)\n\nCase2: 36 pairs * 2 numbers each =72\n\nTotal two-digit numbers: 9 +9 +72=90. But wait, the total two-digit numbers in the entire range (10-99) are 90. However, according to our breakdown, monodigit (9) + two-digit (Case1 and Case2:9+72=81). 9+81=90. Correct.\n\nBut according to our previous total for two-digit numbers (Case1 and Case2 for n=2):\n\nCase1 (n=2):9 numbers\n\nCase2 (n=2):36*2=72 numbers\n\nSo total two-digit numbers with exactly two different digits:9+72=81, which matches.\n\nSimilarly, for n=3:\n\nMonodigit:9 (111,...,999)\n\nCase1: For each d=1-9, 3 numbers. So 9*3=27 numbers (like 100,101,110 for d=1)\n\nCase2: For each pair {a,b},6 numbers. 36 pairs *6=216 numbers\n\nTotal three-digit numbers with at most two digits:9 +27 +216=252\n\nBut how many three-digit numbers are there in total? From 100 to 999:900 numbers. The ones with at most two digits would be 9 (monodigit) + others. Let's see, our calculation gives 252, which seems plausible.\n\nSimilarly, for n=4:\n\nMonodigit:9 (1111,...,9999)\n\nCase1: For each d=1-9,7 numbers. 9*7=63 numbers\n\nCase2: For each pair {a,b},14 numbers.36*14=504 numbers\n\nTotal four-digit numbers with at most two digits:9 +63 +504=576\n\nFour-digit numbers in total: from 1000 to 9999:9000 numbers. 576 is a small fraction, which makes sense.\n\nAdding up all lengths:\n\nMonodigit:9 (n=1) +9 (n=2)+9(n=3)+9(n=4)=36\n\nTwo-digit numbers:\n\nn=2:9 (Case1) +72 (Case2)=81\n\nn=3:27 (Case1)+216 (Case2)=243\n\nn=4:63 (Case1)+504 (Case2)=567\n\nTotal two-digit numbers:81+243+567=891\n\nTotal at most two digits:36+891=927\n\nTherefore, the answer seems to be 927. But 