ces after these operations. So, perhaps the key is that the difference between consecutive elements in each row must be made constant. Let's denote the desired difference for row i as d_i. Then, for each row i, the difference between column j+1 and column j in the final table should be d_i for all j from 1 to 6.\n\nOriginal differences in row i: Let's compute the original difference a_{i,j+1} - a_{i,j}.\n\nGiven a_{i,j} = (i\u00b2 + j)(i + j\u00b2)\n\nSo, the original difference is:\n\n\u0394_{i,j} = a_{i,j+1} - a_{i,j} = [ (i\u00b2 + (j+1))(i + (j+1)^2 ) ] - [ (i\u00b2 + j)(i + j\u00b2 ) ]\n\nLet me compute this expression:\n\nFirst, expand both terms:\n\nFirst term: (i\u00b2 + j + 1)(i + j\u00b2 + 2j + 1)\n\nSecond term: (i\u00b2 + j)(i + j\u00b2)\n\nCompute the difference:\n\n\u0394 = (i\u00b2 + j +1)(i + j\u00b2 + 2j +1) - (i\u00b2 + j)(i + j\u00b2)\n\nLet me expand both products:\n\nFirst product:\n\n(i\u00b2)(i) + i\u00b2(j\u00b2 + 2j +1) + j(i) + j(j\u00b2 + 2j +1) + 1*(i) + 1*(j\u00b2 + 2j +1)\n\n= i\u00b3 + i\u00b2j\u00b2 + 2i\u00b2j + i\u00b2 + ij + j\u00b3 + 2j\u00b2 + j + i + j\u00b2 + 2j +1\n\nSecond product:\n\n(i\u00b2)(i) + i\u00b2j\u00b2 + j(i) + j(j\u00b2)\n\n= i\u00b3 + i\u00b2j\u00b2 + ij + j\u00b3\n\nSubtracting the second product from the first:\n\n\u0394 = [i\u00b3 + i\u00b2j\u00b2 + 2i\u00b2j + i\u00b2 + ij + j\u00b3 + 2j\u00b2 + j + i + j\u00b2 + 2j +1] - [i\u00b3 + i\u00b2j\u00b2 + ij + j\u00b3]\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\ni\u00b3 cancels.\n\ni\u00b2j\u00b2 cancels.\n\n2i\u00b2j remains.\n\ni\u00b2 remains.\n\nij cancels.\n\nj\u00b3 cancels.\n\n2j\u00b2 remains.\n\nj remains.\n\ni remains.\n\nj\u00b2 remains.\n\n2j remains.\n\n1 remains.\n\nSo \u0394 = 2i\u00b2j + i\u00b2 + 2j\u00b2 + j + i + j\u00b2 + 2j +1\n\nCombine like terms:\n\ni\u00b2 terms: 2i\u00b2j + i\u00b2\n\nj\u00b2 terms: 2j\u00b2 + j\u00b2 = 3j\u00b2\n\nj terms: j + 2j = 3j\n\ni term: i\n\nConstants: 1\n\nSo \u0394 = i\u00b2(2j +1) + 3j\u00b2 + 3j + i + 1\n\nTherefore, the original difference between consecutive elements in row i, column j is \u0394 = i\u00b2(2j +1) + 3j\u00b2 + 3j + i +1.\n\nThis seems complicated. The difference itself is a quadratic function in j, which suggests that the original rows are not arithmetic sequences, as the differences are not constant. Therefore, to make each row an arithmetic sequence, we need to adjust these differences to be constant for each row.\n\nEach time we add an arithmetic sequence to a row, we are adding a linear term to the entries of that row. Specifically, adding an arithmetic sequence with common difference d to a row will add d to each difference in that row. For example, if we add a sequence like c, c+d, c+2d, ..., c+6d to a row, the differences between consecutive elements in that row will increase by d each time. Wait, no: actually, the differences of the added sequence are d, so the original differences plus d. For example, if the original differences are \u0394_j, then after adding an arithmetic sequence with difference d, the new differences become \u0394_j + d. Because the added sequence has differences d, so when added to the original row, each original difference \u0394_j = a_{i,j+1} - a_{i,j} becomes (a_{i,j+1} + s_{j+1}) - (a_{i,j} + s_j) = \u0394_j + (s_{j+1} - s_j) = \u0394_j + d.\n\nTherefore, adding an arithmetic sequence to a row changes all the differences in that row by adding the common difference of the arithmetic sequence. Similarly, adding an arithmetic sequence to a column affects the differences in the rows that intersect that column. Wait, how?\n\nIf we add an arithmetic sequence to a column, say column k, then for each row i, the entry a_{i,k} is increased by some term t_i, where t_i is part of an arithmetic sequence. Since it's a column operation, the arithmetic sequence added to column k is of the form t_1, t_2, ..., t_7, which is an arithmetic sequence. So, t_i = c + (i-1)d for some c, d. Therefore, when we add this to column k, each entry in column k gets increased by t_i. How does this affect the differences in the rows?\n\nIn row i, the difference between column j and j+1 will be affected only if one of the columns is k. Specifically, if we add t_i to column k, then the difference between column k-1 and k becomes (a_{i,k} + t_i) - a_{i,k-1}, and the difference between column k and k+1 becomes a_{i,k+1} - (a_{i,k} + t_i). Therefore, adding t_i to column k affects two differences in row i: the difference before column k is increased by t_i, and the difference after column k is decreased by t_i. However, since we're adding an arithmetic sequence to the entire column, t_i = c + (i-1)d. Therefore, each row i has t_i added to column k, which affects the differences in that row as mentioned.\n\nTherefore, adding an arithmetic sequence to a column can adjust the differences in the rows that pass through that column. Specifically, for each row i, the difference between columns k-1 and k is increased by t_i - t_{i-1}? Wait, no. Let me think again.\n\nWait, actually, for a given column k, when we add an arithmetic sequence t_i = c + (i-1)d to the column, this affects the entries in column k across all rows. For a specific row i, the entry at column k becomes a_{i,k} + t_i. Therefore, in row i, the difference between column k and k-1 is:\n\n(a_{i,k} + t_i) - (a_{i,k-1} + t_{i}') ), where t_{i}' is the potential addition from column k-1. Wait, but if column k-1 hasn't been operated on, then t_{i}' is zero. Hmm, this seems complicated because operations on columns can interfere with each other.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to model this as a linear system. Let me consider that each row operation and each column operation contributes to modifying the entries of the table, and we need to solve for the necessary operations to make each row an arithmetic sequence.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet R_i be the arithmetic sequence added to row i. So, R_i(j) = c_i + d_i*(j - 1), for j=1 to 7.\n\nSimilarly, let C_j be the arithmetic sequence added to column j. So, C_j(i) = e_j + f_j*(i - 1), for i=1 to 7.\n\nThen, the final entry in cell (i,j) is:\n\nB_{i,j} = a_{i,j} + R_i(j) + C_j(i)\n\nWe need each row of B to be an arithmetic sequence. That is, for each row i, B_{i,j+1} - B_{i,j} must be constant for all j from 1 to 6.\n\nSimilarly, if we can express B_{i,j} as p_i + q_i*(j - 1), then the difference q_i must be constant across j for each i.\n\nSo, the problem reduces to finding R_i and C_j (arithmetic sequences) such that B_{i,j} is an arithmetic sequence in each row.\n\nThis seems like a system of equations. Let's analyze the conditions.\n\nFor each row i, the difference B_{i,j+1} - B_{i,j} must be constant for all j.\n\nLet's write this difference:\n\nB_{i,j+1} - B_{i,j} = [a_{i,j+1} + R_i(j+1) + C_{j+1}(i)] - [a_{i,j} + R_i(j) + C_j(i)]\n\nSimplify:\n\n= [a_{i,j+1} - a_{i,j}] + [R_i(j+1) - R_i(j)] + [C_{j+1}(i) - C_j(i)]\n\nWe know that R_i is an arithmetic sequence with common difference d_i, so R_i(j+1) - R_i(j) = d_i.\n\nSimilarly, C_{j+1}(i) - C_j(i) = [e_{j+1} + f_{j+1}(i - 1)] - [e_j + f_j(i - 1)]\n\n= (e_{j+1} - e_j) + (f_{j+1} - f_j)(i - 1)\n\nTherefore, the difference B_{i,j+1} - B_{i,j} is:\n\n\u0394_{i,j} + d_i + (e_{j+1} - e_j) + (f_{j+1} - f_j)(i - 1)\n\nAnd this must be equal to the common difference q_i of the arithmetic sequence in row i, which is constant for all j.\n\nTherefore, for each i and j (1 \u2264 j \u2264 6), we have:\n\n\u0394_{i,j} + d_i + (e_{j+1} - e_j) + (f_{j+1} - f_j)(i - 1) = q_i\n\nNote that q_i is independent of j. Therefore, the left-hand side must be independent of j for each fixed i.\n\nGiven that \u0394_{i,j} is a function of i and j (as computed earlier: i\u00b2(2j +1) + 3j\u00b2 + 3j + i +1), and the rest of the terms involve d_i, e_j, and f_j.\n\nThis seems complicated because for each i and j, we have an equation, and we need to solve for variables d_i (7 variables, one for each row), e_j and f_j (for each column j, 1 to 7, but since e_{j+1} - e_